{"reply":"In **Chapter 3, “A Caucus-Race and a Long Tale,”** the Dodo announces: “_Everybody_ has won, and all must have prizes.” When the others ask who should give them out, the Dodo points to Alice and says, “_she_, of course.”

The thimble feels awkward because the “prize” is really Alice’s own leftover possession, returned to her through an elaborate ceremony. The seriousness of the presentation makes the circularity sharper: she supplies the prizes, then receives a token made from what she already had. That seems close to what you’re describing with the lunches—not necessarily that the thanks are insincere, but that being thanked can feel oddly inadequate when the thing being recognised has already cost you money, effort, and organisation. The gesture hands something back while leaving the original burden visible.

